1.	INTRODUCTION – (MARCUS)
With the development of artificial intelligence and machine learning techniques moving from theory to practice, the question of maximizing the effectiveness of the models has become an important engineering task. In a machine learning workflow, the capabilities of a model are controlled not only by the patterns obtained from the training data but also by the parameters which have been defined before the training process itself. Learning how to manage these parameters properly is the key to developing efficient predictive systems.
2.	DESCRIPTION OF EXPERIENCE/TOPIC - (MARCUS)
The experiment was aimed at tuning the RandomForestClassifier that had been trained with the help of the Iris dataset. The process started from creating the baseline model and then proceeded to tuning using manually performed methods as well as completely automated approaches. Firstly, we investigated the method called Grid Search (GridSearchCV). This approach allows testing all possible combinations of the parameters inside a specific area with the use of cross-validation to find the best ones. Secondly, we used the method of Random Search (RandomizedSearchCV). It allowed exploring a significantly larger area of parameters and picking up the optimal configurations at random. Finally, the experiment ended with studying the Automated Machine Learning (AutoML) method with the help of AutoGluon.
3.	PERSONAL REFLECTION – (MARCUS)
Going through this lab has helped me gain practical insights about the immense untapped potential that lies beyond the conventional settings for machine learning algorithms. The whole process of manually implementing the Grid Search and Random Search algorithm helped understand the common tradeoff between comprehensive accuracy and efficiency while searching the parameter space of high dimensions. Seeing AutoGluon do everything from data preparation, feature creation, and ensembling of models in just a few minutes has totally changed my outlook toward workflow automation. This experience made me realize that although the manual approach helps develop an understanding of the process of learning of the model, AutoML is a scaling factor for the same.
